CORE
getopt.sh
--resolve-dynamic-sites --cvc5 --shell-max-arguments 4 --unwind 7
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^\[shell-unsupported\.1\] .* GETOPT_COMPATIBLE is not set where getopt outputs options .*: FAILURE$
^\[shell-argument-count\.1\] .* the output of getopt has at most --shell-max-arguments \(4\) words: FAILURE$
^\[shell-dynamic-site\.1\] .*: SUCCESS$
^\[shell-unsupported\.2\] .* the parts of the text of eval for the holes of "set -- "\* .*: SUCCESS$
^\[assertion\.1\] .* "an option or --": SUCCESS$
^\[\.unwind\.0\] .*: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
getopt's output is made of its words: the options of -o and -l, with
their arguments, then --, then the other parameters, each argument and
parameter quoted for the shell. The words are the fields of a ghost list,
which eval of the output uses rather than parsing it, so that the loop
sees only options and --. The bound on the number of words is a
property, as getopt may output any number of them.
